From 9bdb42f41c25c884ea5725819bd2659cf77d51ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 02:13:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/19] Fix the direct API host, and judge seeds on
 completeness rather than spread

Two corrections, one of them mine to own.

The direct plan's host was guessed and wrong. The official specification
(doc.aerodatabox.com/docs/openapi-direct-v1.yaml) gives the server as
https://api.aerodatabox.com/ with an X-Api-Key header; the code had a made-up
host and a header belonging to a different reseller. The same specification
marks the FIDS endpoint TIER 2, which settles the open question about what a
call costs on the direct plans: two units, the same as measured on RapidAPI.
The path and query parameters were already right.

The larger fix is conceptual. Injecting a known bias into the T-100 harness
shows a per-carrier factor of 1.30x, 2.00x or even 2.80x leaves the share error
at 1.96 pp, identical to no bias at all, because a factor applied evenly across
a carrier's network is a column scaling and the fit cancels it. The same holds
for a per-route factor. Only a bias that differs by carrier *within* a route
survives, and that is not identifiable from the margins.

So the acceptance test was leading with a number that cannot disqualify
anything: it read the pilot at 1.30x and concluded the source failed, when a
bias of that shape costs exactly zero. The verdict now rests on completeness --
a missing carrier, a missing route, a partial network exposed by column_scale --
and the coverage spread is reported as diagnostic. A test pins the invariance
the redesign depends on, so if it ever stops holding the verdict logic fails
loudly rather than quietly licensing a bad source.

